 "authorLine": "FRUS research run, 2026-10-31",
 "introductionText": "What FRUS answers directly: From 1949 to 1980, officials treated American appeal in Asia as an instrument to be aimed, not an asset to be counted. Their main strategy was indirection. Messages went out through Asian governments, delegates and media, with American visibility kept low (1952–1954, Volume XII, Part 2, Document 403; 1955–1957, Volume XXI, Document 23; 1917–1972, Volume VIII, Document 31). They pictured the audience as nationalist, alert to race and to colonial memory, and moved by emotion more than argument (1955–1957, Volume XXII, Document 58; 1964–1968, Volume XXIX, Part 2, Document 46). They rarely claimed success. They judged effect by attendance and by events, and repeatedly concluded that deeds outweighed words (1964–1968, Volume I, Document 197; 1917–1972, Volume VI, Document 126). After Saigon fell, the question became the credibility of American commitment (1969–1976, Volume XXXVIII, Part 2, Document 105). The finding rests on twenty-two documents, read whole, from eighty-six volumes.\n\nWhere FRUS points for the rest: Series the printed documents came from: - Records Relating to National Security Council Policy Papers (Executive Secretariat), Record Group 59, entry A1 1586E, NARA identifier 2839192, 1947–1979, partly restricted; holds the information-program progress reports to the National Security Council and the Thailand plan's file. - Subject and Special Files (Operations Coordinating Board), Record Group 59, entry A1 1586C, NARA identifier 2839191, 1953–1961, partly restricted; holds the country plans such as Burma's. - Department of State Central Files (decimal file, then the subject-numeric file from 1963, then the Central Foreign Policy File), Record Group 59; the bundled indexes give no NARA identifier for these years. - National Security Files, Vietnam Country Files, Johnson Library, NARA identifier 591194, 1963–1969; access not recorded in the bundled indexes. - White House Central Files, Confidential Files, Johnson Library, NARA identifier 12005682, 1963–1969; access not recorded. - U.S. Information Agency, Director's Subject Files, 1968–1972, Record Group 306, entry A1–42; no NARA identifier in the bundled indexes. - U.S. Information Agency, Office of Policy and Plans subject files, Record Group 306, entry P–207; no NARA identifier in the bundled indexes. - U.S. Information Agency, Office of the Director correspondence, Record Group 306 (the edition prints \"RG 305\"), accession 306–84–0003, which the bundled index splits between Correspondence Files (NARA identifier 5552237) and Memorandums Pertaining to the Executive Committee (5585931); dates and access not recorded. - Plains Files, Carter Library, collection NARA identifier 591256, probably its Subject Files (591289), 1973–1982; I matched this series by title only. Series the editors cited without printing: - Psychological Strategy Board Working Files, Record Group 59, entry A1 1462, NARA identifier 2124692, 1951–1953, possibly restricted; holds the drafts of the Thailand psychological strategy. - U.S. Information Agency, Office of Policy and Plans, Subject Files, Record Group 306, entries UD-WW 151 to UD-WW 382, NARA identifier 1257679, 1953–1971, partly restricted; the editors cite its files on the Suez crisis and on nuclear testing. - U.S. Information Agency files retired as lots 60 D 322 and 62 D 255; the bundled indexes give no NARA identifier. - Department of State Central Files, the information-program file of 1952–1955 (Record Group 59); among it, the other posts' replies to the 1952 circular asking ambassadors to judge their information programs, of which FRUS prints one.\n\nWhat this request cannot get from FRUS: The phrase \"soft power\" appears in none of the 553 volumes; I confirmed the search worked with a phrase found in every volume and a nonsense control found in none. So the question has to be asked in the period's own words, as I did. Audience research is the larger gap. I searched for opinion polls and opinion or attitude surveys alongside the information agency across all eighty-six volumes, with the same two controls. I found fewer than thirty documents, and only one of them in a regional volume. The polls themselves, and the agency's country plans and post reports, stay in the information agency's own records listed above. Asian audiences never speak for themselves in this record. What Asians thought reaches FRUS only through American reporting.",
